# Twenty verified scholarly works. Concise paraphrases; full links retained.
refs=[
('Ibidunmoye, Hernandez-Rodriguez and Elmroth (2015). Performance Anomaly Detection and Bottleneck Identification. ACM Computing Surveys.','https://doi.org/10.1145/2791120','Surveys performance anomaly detection and bottleneck analysis.','Supports telemetry analysis; does not validate ARQ thresholds.'),
('Liu, Ting and Zhou (2008). Isolation Forest. ICDM.','https://www.lamda.nju.edu.cn/publication/icdm08b.pdf','Uses isolation trees to score unusual observations.','ARQ uses a standard optional model in shadow mode.'),
('Hochreiter and Schmidhuber (1997). Long Short-Term Memory. Neural Computation.','https://doi.org/10.1162/neco.1997.9.8.1735','Introduces recurrent modeling of temporal dependencies.','Background only; no trained LSTM is established here.'),
('Malhotra et al. (2016). LSTM-based Encoder-Decoder for Multi-sensor Anomaly Detection.','https://arxiv.org/abs/1607.00148','Detects unusual sequences through reconstruction error.','Sequence reconstruction is prior work, not a new ARQ mechanism.'),
('Somayaji (2002). Operating System Stability and Security through Process Homeostasis. Doctoral thesis.','https://www.cs.unm.edu/~forrest/dissertations/smoayaji-dissertation.pdf','Explores behavioral process monitoring and adaptive response.','A foundational overlap with observe-and-respond systems.'),
('Lo et al. (2016). Improving Resource Efficiency at Scale with Heracles. ACM TOCS.','https://research.google/pubs/improving-resource-efficiency-at-scale-with-heracles/','Manages colocated resources with feedback to protect latency.','Latency-driven allocation and recovery are established.'),
('Fried et al. (2020). Caladan: Mitigating Interference at Microsecond Timescales. OSDI.','https://www.usenix.org/conference/osdi20/presentation/fried','Uses fast allocation and contention signals for isolation.','ARQ does not reproduce its scheduler or timing scale.'),
('Chen, Delimitrou and Martinez (2019). PARTIES: QoS-Aware Resource Partitioning for Multiple Interactive Services. ASPLOS.','https://www.csl.cornell.edu/~martinez/doc/asplos19.pdf','Dynamically partitions resources for colocated interactive services.','Multi-service QoS feedback is not novel to this project.'),
('Ousterhout et al. (2019). Shenango: Achieving High CPU Efficiency for Latency-sensitive Datacenter Workloads. NSDI.','https://www.usenix.org/conference/nsdi19/presentation/ousterhout','Adjusts CPU allocations to changing service demands.','Provides a scheduling comparison, not an ARQ implementation.'),
('Zhang et al. (2013). CPI2: CPU performance isolation for shared compute clusters. EuroSys.','https://research.google/pubs/cpi2-cpu-performance-isolation-for-shared-compute-clusters/','Uses CPI observations to identify interference and throttle likely offenders.','Interference-aware selective throttling already exists.'),
('Delimitrou and Kozyrakis (2014). Quasar: Resource-Efficient and QoS-Aware Cluster Management. ASPLOS.','https://www.csl.cornell.edu/~delimitrou/papers/2014.asplos.quasar.pdf','Combines classification with resource allocation and assignment.','Learned resource decisions are established research.'),
('Delimitrou and Kozyrakis (2013). Paragon: QoS-aware scheduling for heterogeneous datacenters. ASPLOS.','https://mast.stanford.edu/pubs/paragon_qos_aware_scheduling_for_heterogeneous_datacenters/','Accounts for hardware heterogeneity and workload interference.','A broader placement problem than local CPU-quarantine release.'),
('Mars et al. (2011). Bubble-Up: Increasing Utilization In Modern Warehouse Scale Computers Via Sensible Co-Locations. MICRO.','https://research.google/pubs/bubble-up-increasing-utilization-in-modern-warehouse-scale-computers-via-sensible-co-locations/','Applies tunable memory pressure to characterize interference sensitivity.','Active perturbation for interference assessment is known.'),
('Banga, Druschel and Mogul (1999). Resource Containers: A New Facility for Resource Management in Server Systems. OSDI.','https://www.usenix.org/conference/osdi-99/presentation/resource-containers-new-facility-resource-management-server-systems','Separates resource accounting principals from protection domains.','Resource management and security isolation are distinct.'),
('Hindman et al. (2011). Mesos: A Platform for Fine-Grained Resource Sharing in the Data Center. NSDI.','https://www.usenix.org/conference/nsdi11/mesos-platform-fine-grained-resource-sharing-data-center','Coordinates fine-grained sharing across cluster frameworks.','Cluster allocation differs from this local recovery experiment.'),
('Verma et al. (2015). Large-scale cluster management at Google with Borg. EuroSys.','https://research.google.com/pubs/archive/43438.pdf','Describes large-scale workload placement and cluster management.','Deployment scale and guarantees are not comparable to ARQ.'),
('Schwarzkopf et al. (2013). Omega: flexible, scalable schedulers for large compute clusters. EuroSys.','https://research.google/pubs/omega-flexible-scalable-schedulers-for-large-compute-clusters/','Uses shared state and optimistic concurrency for scheduling.','Does not establish this prototype as a cluster scheduler.'),
('Ghodsi et al. (2011). Dominant Resource Fairness: Fair Allocation of Multiple Resource Types. NSDI.','https://www.usenix.org/conference/nsdi11/dominant-resource-fairness-fair-allocation-multiple-resource-types','Defines multi-resource fair allocation.','ARQ does not implement DRF or prove allocation fairness.'),
('Rzadca et al. (2020). Autopilot: Workload Autoscaling at Google Scale. EuroSys.','https://research.google/pubs/autopilot-workload-autoscaling-at-google-scale/','Automates resource sizing and scaling for workloads.','Autoscaling evidence cannot be transferred to ARQ.'),
('Kulkarni et al. (2019). Pliant: Leveraging Approximation to Improve Datacenter Resource Efficiency. HPCA.','https://www.csl.cornell.edu/~nsk49/publication/pliant_hpca/','Uses approximation to reduce colocation contention.','ARQ changes CPU duty, not application approximation quality.')]

cover('REVIEW I','Problem definition, literature and gaps in existing systems')
page('Review I: abstract and introduction');h('1. Abstract')
para('Operating systems share CPU time, memory and input/output resources among running processes. Sustained resource pressure can make other applications less responsive, but high utilization alone does not show that a process is faulty. Adaptive Resource Quarantine (ARQ) explores a graduated response between passive observation and termination. A Python monitor collects process telemetry, a rule engine combines resource signals and EWMA tracking, and a controller applies staged restrictions through scheduling priority and signal-based CPU duty cycles. The original recovery path reduces restrictions after repeated low scores. This combined report retains that project direction and extends its evaluation to a specific limitation: throttling can itself create the low readings used to infer recovery. An opt-in research module therefore tests a temporary allocation increase between two restricted control windows. It checks protected-service latency, measurement stability and CPU consumption relative to the larger grant before approving one relaxation. Functional tests and constructed scenarios support the implemented logic; they do not establish real-world detection accuracy, production safety or patentability.')
h('2. Introduction');para('ARQ studies a practical operating-systems question: how should a controller respond when a process consumes resources unusually? A brief compiler burst can be legitimate, while sustained excessive demand may disrupt neighboring work. A response should therefore be proportional to the evidence and explicit about its limits.')
para('This report separates three stages. Review I establishes the problem and related work. Review II describes the architecture, implementation and recorded outputs. Review III evaluates the available evidence, compares the mechanisms with existing approaches and identifies future work. A separate IDF-B section records the candidate research mechanism for professional review.')
page('Review I: problem and objectives');h('3. Problem Statement');para('Combine process telemetry and interpretable scoring with graduated execution restrictions and a justified method for relaxing those restrictions. The controller must distinguish a workload that has genuinely reduced demand from one whose observed usage is low only because the controller is restricting it.')
h('4. Objectives')
for t in ['Collect CPU, resident/virtual memory, growth, I/O and thread observations where the host exposes them.','Use the existing resource scoring path to select monitoring and quarantine states.','Apply supported priority and signal-based duty controls, with restoration and cleanup behavior visible to the operator.','Evaluate Isolation Forest separately in shadow mode without treating its scores as calibrated probabilities.','Test a bracketed release-verification policy before a proposed CPU-duty relaxation.','Retain test logs, modeled scenarios and reported historical outputs with clear evidence boundaries.']:para(t)
h('5. Literature Survey');para('The following twenty scholarly works cover anomaly detection, process response, interference assessment and resource management. They are a technical comparison rather than an assertion that earlier systems lack recovery or adaptive control. The studies have not been run head-to-head against ARQ. References are listed after Review III.')
for start in range(0,20,5):
 page('Review I: literature '+str(start+1)+'-'+str(start+5));h('5.'+str(start//5+1)+' Literature Comparison')
 table(['Study','Main contribution','Relevance and boundary'],[[f'[{i+1}] '+refs[i][0],refs[i][2],refs[i][3]] for i in range(start,start+5)],[185,155,168])
page('Review I: gaps');h('6. Gaps and Project Scope')
for sub,text in [('6.1 Fixed thresholds and changing workload phases','A fixed resource threshold is simple to interpret but may treat legitimate bursts as anomalies. ARQ adds burst handling and EWMA tracking, although several component scores still use configured fixed thresholds. Adaptive tracking should not be described as complete workload understanding.'),('6.2 Detection and response','Detection alone does not control resource use. ARQ connects observations to concrete actions, but signal-based throttling is execution control, not a security sandbox or complete memory/I/O isolation.'),('6.3 Restriction-masked recovery','The controller can observe low CPU usage because it reduced runnable time. Repeated low observations therefore need additional evidence before they justify restoration. This is the specific failure mode addressed by the research extension.'),('6.4 Existing feedback systems','Heracles, CPI2, PARTIES and related systems already manage interference and protect workloads [6-10]. The candidate contribution must be assessed as an exact method, not as the first adaptive quarantine or latency-aware controller.'),('6.5 Repeatable evaluation','An interface demonstration is not a detection-accuracy study. Labelled workloads, repeated trials, trustworthy CPU accounting and application-state checks are required before stronger performance or preservation claims are made.')]:h(sub);para(text)

cover('REVIEW II','Proposed methodology, architecture and implementation outputs')
page('Review II: methodology');h('7. Proposed Methodology');para('The base monitoring cycle reads process telemetry, updates the profile, computes a rule score, chooses a stage, applies its associated action and observes the next cycle. Isolation Forest is evaluated alongside that path. Its output is displayed separately and does not select the quarantine stage.')
h('7.1 Resource signals');table(['Signal','Use and limitation'],[['CPU utilization','Sigmoid score around a configured threshold; usage is affected by the current restriction.'],['Memory growth / RSS','Growth and absolute footprint contribute to a bounded score; CPU throttling is not a hard RSS limit.'],['I/O throughput','Read/write rate contributes where the operating system exposes it.'],['Burst deviation','Difference between CPU observation and its EWMA contributes to burst scoring.'],['Thread / virtual memory','Additional features for the optional Isolation Forest pipeline.']],[145,363])
h('7.2 Rule path');para('The default weighted score uses CPU, memory, I/O and burst weights of 0.40, 0.35, 0.15 and 0.10. Source code also applies a dominant-vector boost and early-cycle suppression. The CPU sigmoid denominator is 10 in the inspected source; the EWMA factor is 0.25. These constants require workload-specific evaluation.')
h('7.3 State and configuration');para('Controller defaults use soft/hard thresholds of 0.65/0.85, while the existing demo and benchmark use 0.55/0.75. The report keeps these configurations distinct. The original restoration default requires four low-score cycles.')
page('Review II: architecture');h('8. System Architecture');figure('base');table(['Component','Implementation responsibility'],[['ProcessMonitor','Collect telemetry through native macOS/Linux interfaces.'],['AnomalyScorer','Maintain EWMA tracking and compute the rule score.'],['RestorationEngine','Arbitrate escalation, hysteresis and optional persistent-offender termination.'],['QuarantineController','Apply priority changes and manage duty-cycle pulser threads.'],['Isolation Forest','Provide an optional shadow signal when an artifact is available.'],['Dashboard / CLI','Expose state and run supported demonstrations and evaluations.']],[155,353])
page('Review II: workflow');h('9. Workflow and Graduated States');table(['State','Action and boundary'],[['Normal','Monitor without quarantine restrictions.'],['Watchlist','Record elevated behavior; no CPU-duty restriction.'],['Soft quarantine','Request nice +10 and 40% runnable duty.'],['Hard quarantine','Request nice +19 and 15% runnable duty.'],['Terminated','When configured conditions persist, attempt SIGTERM and then SIGKILL.']],[145,363])
para('Priority operations depend on permissions. Returning from a larger nice value to a lower value may fail for an unprivileged process; the current code does not turn that failure into a guaranteed priority restoration. Signal pulsing pauses and resumes execution but does not erase application memory or prove that external resources remain consistent.')
h('9.1 Original recovery');para('The legacy engine steps down after repeated low scores. This avoids reacting to one sample but does not distinguish suppressed usage from reduced demand. Termination remains a possible policy action, so a general claim that ARQ never kills processes is incorrect.')
h('9.2 Proposed research path');para('The new module is opt-in and operates on one already quarantined process. It tests one larger duty allocation with restricted-release-restricted windows. It does not replace the default dashboard restoration path.')
page('Review II: implementation');h('10. Implementation Details');table(['Source area','Role'],[['arq/core/monitor.py','Native telemetry; current macOS tests crash on this host.'],['arq/core/anomaly_scorer.py','Rule-based composite score and profile updates.'],['arq/core/quarantine_controller.py','Signal pulser, priority changes and cleanup.'],['arq/core/restoration_engine.py','Existing score-driven stage transitions.'],['arq/core/ml_pipeline.py / ml_training.py','Feature extraction, optional model interfaces, training and calibration.'],['arq/web/server.py','ThreadingHTTPServer dashboard backend; not Flask.'],['arq/core/recovery_probe.py','New window contract, policy gates, trace and throttler adapter.'],['research/recovery_probe/evaluate.py','Deterministic synthetic comparison with the legacy policy.']],[210,298])
para('The earlier continued report called the dashboard Flask-based. The inspected repository uses Python ThreadingHTTPServer; this combined report corrects that description. Isolation Forest is an optional shadow component. A sequence-model interface exists, but no trained LSTM deployment or LSTM accuracy is established.')
page('Review II: research implementation');h('10.1 Bracketed Recovery Verification');figure('probe');para('A is the current restricted duty allocation. B is one proposed relaxation. Each B measurement is surrounded by A measurements so that changing control conditions can make the experiment inconclusive rather than trigger restoration. The runner reapplies A in a finally block before a positive decision is committed.')
para('The policy checks process incarnation, finite healthy observations, sample count, reported duty and duration, offered-load stability, control latency drift, absolute and relative protected-service p95, and CPU seconds normalized by the B grant. An adapter changes an existing ARQ throttler without changing its stage or niceness.')
page('Review II: policy');h('10.2 Default Research Policy');table(['Parameter','Default'],[['Control / proposed duty in evaluation','0.15 / 0.40'],['Window length / complete rounds','0.5 seconds / 2 A-B-A rounds'],['Nominal additional grant budget','0.30 one-core seconds per call'],['Protected-service p95 SLO','20 ms'],['Permitted release-induced p95 increase','2 ms'],['Permitted A-window p95 drift','1 ms'],['Permitted offered-load drift','10%'],['Maximum B grant-normalized CPU consumption','0.70'],['Minimum latency observations per window','20']],[330,178])
para('The budget reserves (B - A) x window length x rounds: 0.25 nominal additional core-seconds for the evaluation settings. This is a reservation for requested allocation, not a real-time execution guarantee. A delayed callback may overrun a window before the runner can reject its measurement.')
h('10.3 Required live adapter behavior');para('Live use needs process-creation identity checks, measured effective actuation, bounded sampling, a session lock excluding other controllers, an external watchdog and a cumulative repeated-probe budget. Those deployment components are not implemented by the new module. No live performance result is claimed for it.')
page('Review II: historical dashboard');h('10.4 Supplied Dashboard Output');
img=ROOT/'docs/report_assets/page10-0-Image52.png';story.append(Image(str(img),width=508,height=508*686/1355));story.append(Spacer(1,12))
para('Figure 3. Historical dashboard screenshot supplied in the earlier ARQ Review II report. It is included as the project interface record, not a fresh run of the recovery-probe extension.')
para('The interface presents process resource use, rule scores, shadow-mode ML output and quarantine states. It provides a demonstration surface; the interface itself does not validate resource isolation, restoration correctness or anomaly accuracy.')
h('10.5 Reproducible commands');story.append(Preformatted('python -m unittest tests.test_recovery_probe -v\nPYTHONPATH=. python research/recovery_probe/evaluate.py\npython -m arq.cli demo\npython -m arq.cli benchmark --duration 6.0 --markdown',mono));para('The first two commands reproduce the new tests and synthetic evidence. The last two belong to the existing live prototype and require a validated telemetry environment. Do not treat the historical screenshot as proof that they passed on the current host.')
page('Review II: current outputs');h('11. Current Functional Output');story.append(Preformatted('\n'.join(textwrap.fill(line,96,subsequent_indent='  ') if line else '' for line in (ROOT/'research/recovery_probe/results/probe-tests.log').read_text().splitlines()),mono));para('The log above is the retained execution output for the new module. Tests use simulated observations and controlled/fake actuators. They do not send signals to user workloads or validate native telemetry.')
page('Review II: trace');h('11.1 Example Decision Trace');table(['Observation','Decision implication'],[['A: duty 0.15, CPU consumption 0.15 cores','Low absolute usage alone is compatible with a fully saturated restriction.'],['B: duty 0.40, CPU consumption 0.40 cores','Normalized consumption = 1.0; exceeds the 0.70 recovery gate.'],['Second A: duty 0.15','Restriction is reinstated before the decision is evaluated.'],['Stable load and protected latency','Necessary checks, but insufficient when CPU demand remains masked.'],['Result: restriction_masked_demand','Retain A. Do not mark the process as recovered.']],[245,263])
para('This is a worked example of the explicit synthetic model, not an operating-system trace. The raw evaluation file retains all 500 seeded trials, reasons and final duties. If normalized-demand checking is removed, the hidden-demand cases pass in this model, showing why the gate matters under these assumptions.')
