# IDF-B: ten substantive sections, kept separate from the academic review.
cover('INVENTION DISCLOSURE FORMAT - IDF B','Candidate technical disclosure for institutional and patent-agent review')
para('Draft technical content following the supplied IDF-B section order. Institutional document control, applicant details and inventor declarations must be confirmed by VIT. This is not a filed patent application or a grant.')
page('IDF 1-2');h('1. Title of the invention');para('Bracketed Verification of CPU-Quarantine Relaxation Using Grant-Normalized Consumption and Protected-Service Stability Checks')
h('2. Field / Area of invention');para('Computer science and engineering; operating-system resource control, process scheduling, performance isolation and monitored restoration of restricted process execution.')
h('Disclosure scope');para('The candidate is the new opt-in recovery-verification protocol, not the broad idea of adaptive quarantine. It is implemented as a serialized callable experiment with a bridge to an existing ARQ throttler. It is not enabled in default dashboard enforcement.')
h('Inventor / applicant record');para('The project team consists of Rithika.S (24BCE2984) and K. Vaishnavi (24BCT0183), as supplied in the course report. This does not establish legal inventorship. Human conception, ownership, applicant details, institutional policy and the role of AI assistance need separate confirmation.')
page('IDF 3');h('3. Prior Patents and Publications');table(['Reference','Established overlap','Candidate boundary'],[['US7530072B1 (2009)','Quarantine suspicious threads, reduce CPU and priority, restore after exit criteria.','Broad quarantine-and-recovery claims overlap strongly.'],['US9280662B2 (2016)','Keep misbehaving processes operating in isolation and perform remediation.','Candidate tests relaxation of an existing restriction rather than migration.'],['Heracles [6]; PARTIES [8]','Feedback allocation using latency or QoS observations.','Latency-aware relaxation alone is insufficient as a distinction.'],['CPI2 [10]; Bubble-Up [13]','Interference diagnosis, throttling and active resource perturbation.','Active experimentation and selective control are known.'],['US20230128059A1 (2023)','Dynamic resources for latency-sensitive application SLOs.','Full claims and patent-family comparison remain necessary.'],['cpulimit','Duty-cycle CPU control using stop/continue signals.','The actuator is not claimed as new.']],[150,180,178])
para('This comparison is preliminary. The exact combination must be checked against full disclosures, patent families and non-patent literature. No search result establishes novelty merely because its title differs. Expired patents may still constitute prior art.')
page('IDF 4');h('4. Summary of the Invention');para('A resource controller retains a restricted allocation A for a process whose observed usage may be low because of that restriction. Before relaxing it to B, the controller reserves a configured nominal additional grant and performs repeated A-B-A observation groups. It records CPU consumption, the effective allocation, protected-service latency and offered load for each group. The restriction is reapplied before the decision is evaluated.')
para('The proposal is rejected or treated as inconclusive when observations are invalid, the process identity changes, offered load varies too much, restricted-control latency drifts, protected latency exceeds absolute or relative bounds, or CPU consumption substantially saturates the larger grant. A successful decision permits only the tested allocation step. It does not certify full recovery or application correctness.')
h('Technical problem');para('A score-only recovery rule can mistake the controller-induced reduction in observed CPU use for reduced underlying demand. This can trigger premature relaxation. The proposed experiment supplies additional intervention and protected-workload observations rather than relying on repeated low restricted scores alone.')
h('Technical effect to investigate');para('More selective relaxation of CPU restrictions under an explicit measurement contract. Current tests and synthetic evidence show the intended control behavior; live resource savings, response-time improvement, safety and generalization have not been established.')
page('IDF 5');h('5. Objectives of the Invention');
for t in ['Verify one proposed CPU-duty relaxation through repeated restricted-release-restricted observations.','Check whether the candidate grant remains saturated, rather than interpreting low restricted CPU as recovery.','Evaluate the protected workload against absolute latency and relative-degradation gates.','Reject comparisons affected by offered-load change or restricted-control drift.','Reserve a nominal incremental grant before the experiment, and retain the original restriction on rejection or invalid data.','Keep phase observations and reasons in an auditable decision trace.']:para(t)
h('Scope boundary');para('The implementation addresses one-core normalized CPU consumption and one protected-service observation contract. It does not solve general resource attribution, multicore normalization, memory isolation or application-state restoration. The thresholds are configurable engineering bounds, not derived optimal settings.')
page('IDF 6');h('6. Components and Working Principle');table(['Component','Function'],[['Window','Immutable observation contract: process identity, duty, duration, CPU time, latency samples, rate and health.'],['ProbePolicy','Configurable measurement, demand, latency, drift and budget limits.'],['RecoveryProbe','Run bracketed experiment; validate observations; restore before deciding; commit one step only on success.'],['ProbeResult','Decision, reason, final duty, nominal reservation and phase trace.'],['ExistingThrottlerAdapter','Update an already quarantined ARQ duty-cycle pulser and profile allocation.'],['Caller-supplied sampler / serialization','Provide trustworthy measurements and prevent concurrent control writers; live components remain external.']],[155,353])
h('Working principle');para('Check the reservation before actuation. For each required round: apply A and observe; apply B and observe; return to A and observe. Validate the three-window comparison. Always reapply A on exit from the experiment. If all rounds pass, apply B as the final allocation; otherwise retain A. Callback or rollback failures propagate as exceptions rather than success results.')
page('IDF 7 diagram');h('7. Description of the Invention in Detail');figure('probe');h('7.1 Observation and validity contract');para('The process identity must identify an incarnation, such as PID plus creation identity. Each window must provide finite healthy metrics, sufficient protected requests, a positive offered rate, nonnegative CPU/latency values and agreement with the requested duration and duty. The prototype trusts the callback to obtain these measurements; a nominal requested ratio alone is not verified actual actuation.')
h('7.2 Control and demand gates');para('Control-window p95 drift must stay within 1 ms, offered-rate variation within 10%, and all p95 values within the 20 ms SLO. The probe p95 increase over the median A-window p95 must remain within 2 ms. Probe CPU seconds divided by duration times duty must remain at most 0.70. All values are the default research policy and can be changed.')
page('IDF 7 algorithm');h('7.3 Algorithm');story.append(Preformatted('reserve = (B - A) * window_seconds * rounds\nif reserve > budget: return budget_exceeded\ntry:\n    for each required round:\n        before = apply_and_observe(A)\n        trial  = apply_and_observe(B)\n        after  = apply_and_observe(A)\n        validate identity, samples, duty, duration, health\n        check absolute SLO for each window\n        reject changed offered load or A-window drift\n        reject excessive B latency increase\n        reject saturated grant-normalized B consumption\nfinally:\n    apply(A)\nif every complete round passed:\n    try: apply(B)\n    on failure: apply(A); raise\n    return verified_step\nreturn retained_restriction_with_reason',mono))
h('7.4 Limits of the experiment');para('A-B-A bracketing does not prove causality in the presence of unobserved changes or delayed effects. The method cannot prove that a blocked or externally contended process has low latent demand. It does not bound an unresponsive callback, survive daemon death or guarantee signal delivery. Production use needs a watchdog, process-identity validation, synchronization, repeated-probe limits and actual actuation accounting.')
h('7.5 Integration boundary');para('The new module does not run concurrently with the legacy restoration engine by design contract. The caller must serialize both paths. A positive result changes duty only; stage and niceness remain unchanged. No normal-state declaration, memory restoration or ML-based approval is performed.')
page('IDF 8');h('8. Experimental Validation Results');table(['Evidence','Result and interpretation'],[['New regression methods','12 passed; simulated observations and fake/controlled actuators.'],['Other passing modules','20 methods across scorer, inference interface, quarantine and restoration.'],['Broader validation','ML training dependency unavailable; native macOS monitor process crashed.'],['Constructed recovered cases','100/100 probe approvals under the stated model.'],['Constructed masked-demand cases','0/100 probe approvals; legacy relaxed in 100/100.'],['Constructed protected harm / drift / load change','0/100 approvals in each scenario; invalid comparisons are inconclusive.'],['Demand-gate ablation','100/100 masked-demand approvals when that gate is removed.']],[205,303])
para('The five modeled groups contain 500 seeded trials. Their outcomes exercise programmed gates, not a representative labelled detection dataset. They cannot establish real-world false-positive rates or improved tail latency. Thirty-two passing methods do not make the entire suite successful.')
h('Disclosure and publication record');para('The base repository was publicly accessible when reviewed. Its commit timestamps do not establish first public availability. This report and the new extension are being prepared for repository publication at the user request on 30 October 2026; publication is a disclosure event to record if the push succeeds. Any earlier filing, priority date or applicable exception must be assessed by an Indian patent agent. Making a repository private later would not erase earlier disclosure.')
page('IDF 9');h('9. Aspects Proposed for Protection');para('The following is a candidate claim skeleton for review, not an examined claim set. Resource quarantine, throttling, feedback control, intervention experiments and defensive cleanup each have prior art. Novelty and inventive step of the proposed conjunction remain unresolved.')
